<BaseLayout
  title="Epithalon: One Research Group's Evidence — Nawtch"
  description="Epithalon's telomerase and longevity claims come almost entirely from one Russian lab — unapproved, unreplicated for decades."
  canonicalPath="/supplements/peptides/epithalon/"
  bodyClass="diets"
>
      <header>
        <div class="header-left">
          <h1>Epithalon (Epitalon)</h1>
          <p class="subtitle">Telomerase and longevity claims trace almost entirely to one research group — not independently established.</p>
        </div>
        <a class="ghost-btn" href="/supplements/peptides/">← Back to Peptides</a>
      </header>

      <div class="settings-section" style="border:1px solid var(--accent); background:rgba(200,240,96,0.08);">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title" style="color:var(--accent);">Last Verified: August 14, 2026</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section" style="border:1px solid var(--warn); background:rgba(240,96,96,0.08);">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title" style="color:var(--danger);">Tier 3 — Unapproved Research Chemical</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            Epithalon is sold online as a research chemical, is not FDA-approved for any use, and <strong>its core scientific claims come almost entirely from a single research
            group</strong>, not a broad independently-replicated evidence base. This page
            describes what's known — <strong>not dosing or usage guidance, and not a
            recommendation to use it.</strong> Talk to a doctor before considering it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            Epithalon (also spelled Epitalon; research code AEDG, for its four constituent amino
            acids) is a synthetic tetrapeptide based on epithalamin, a pineal-gland extract
            studied for decades by Russian researcher Dr. Vladimir Khavinson and colleagues. Its
            headline claimed mechanism is activating telomerase, the enzyme that rebuilds the
            protective telomere caps on chromosomes — the basis for its marketing around aging and
            longevity.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">What Research Actually Exists — and Its Central Limitation</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            Nearly every preclinical and clinical study behind Epithalon's reputation was
            conducted by the Khavinson group itself, without independent confirmation from
            unaffiliated labs — the single biggest limitation on how much confidence the evidence
            base can support. The foundational claim that Epithalon activates telomerase rests on
            a 2003 in-vitro study from that group reporting induced telomerase activity and
            telomere elongation in cultured human fibroblasts, without the kind of detailed
            statistical reporting (fold-changes, confidence intervals) that would let outside
            researchers fully evaluate it. A 2011 attempt by a separate US research group to
            replicate the telomerase-activation finding, using commercially synthesized AEDG,
            found no significant effect. More recent independent work (Al-Dulaimi et al., 2025)
            reported telomere-length increases through multiple pathways, offering some outside
            validation — but the overall picture remains a research area still working toward
            reliable independent replication, not settled science.
          </p>
          <br />
          <a href="https://www.alzdiscovery.org/uploads/cognitive_vitality_media/Epithalamin-and-Epithalon-Cognitive-Vitality-For-Researchers.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Alzheimer's Drug Discovery Foundation — Cognitive Vitality Report on Epithalamin/Epithalon →</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">Current Legal &amp; Regulatory Status</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <p class="settings-desc">
            Epithalon has never been FDA-approved for any use in the United States. It was one of
            the peptides the FDA removed from its Category 2 "significant safety risk" compounding
            list effective April 22, 2026 — not approval, only a change in nomination status — and
            in the July 23–24, 2026 advisory committee meeting, the committee voted to recommend
            Epitalon for the compounding-permitted list, a recommendation that still requires
            formal FDA rulemaking (typically 8–24 months) before it would actually become legal
            to compound. Until then, it remains available only through unregulated
            research-chemical sellers.
          </p>
          <br />
          <a href="https://www.fda.gov/drugs/human-drug-compounding/bulk-drug-substances-used-compounding-under-section-503a-fdc-act" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FDA — Bulk Drug Substances Used in Compounding →</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="settings-section">
        <h2 class="settings-section-title">Learn More</h2>
        <div class="settings-section-body">
          <a href="https://www.alzdiscovery.org/uploads/cognitive_vitality_media/Epithalamin-and-Epithalon-Cognitive-Vitality-For-Researchers.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Alzheimer's Drug Discovery Foundation — Epithalamin and Epithalon: Cognitive Vitality For Researchers</a>
        </div>
      </div>
</BaseLayout>
